### 6.6 Probing the first-order phase transition

#### Proposed theoretical prediction

The theoretical attempts to study the first-order phase transition and QCD phase structure at large \(\mu_{B}\), in general, are quite challenging due to the issue of sign problem encountered by first-principle calculations, such as from LQCD. Hence experimental evidence of first-order phase transition is very crucial.

Various model studies have been done to aid in the search for first-order phase transition. One such study exploits the two-component/bimodal nature of proton multiplicity distribution near a first-order phase transition. A first-order phase transition is characterized by the presence of a mixed phase. The multiplicity distribution of charged particles, such as protons, could develop a two-component/bimodal structure due to contributions from the two distinct phases constituting the mixed phase. An example of a two-component distribution is shown in Fig 6.17. Mathematically a two-component distribution (\(P(N)\)) can be expressed as follows;

\[P(N)=(1-\alpha)P_{A}(N)+\alpha P_{B}(N), \tag{6.5}\]where \(B_{-}(N)\) and \(B_{-}(N)\) are the probability distribution of the two constituents, and the parameter \(\alpha\) ( such that \(\alpha\leq 1\)) specifies their relative contribution.

The two-component structure could lead to an interesting behavior of factorial cumulants. Constructing a two-component distribution comprising of a Binomial and Poissonian by taking inputs from experimental data on proton factorial cumulants up to fourth-order, the authors of Ref. [26] found that the resulting distribution is characterized by a large value of higher-order factorial cumulants. Not only were they large, but with increasing order, the factorial cumulants rapidly rose in magnitude and alternated their signs [26]. These features of factorial cumulants are utilized in the experimental search for first-order phase transition.

